/*
Since the provided statement is incomplete and does not include a formal
input specification, we implement the standard interpretation of the task:

- There are n runners.
- Runner i completes one lap in t[i] minutes.
- The training lasts T minutes.
- A runner needs one water container each time they pass the start point.
- Water is replenished every 60 minutes, so containers are only needed for
  the maximum number of simultaneous requirements within any 60-minute block.

Under this interpretation, the minimum number of containers required equals
the maximum, over all hour-long segments, of the total number of lap
completions by all runners in that segment.

For a runner with lap time p, the maximum number of completions in any
interval of length 60 is ceil(60 / p), and over total duration T we only
count within the actually existing time, so for T < 60 it becomes
max completions in any interval of length T, i.e. floor(T / p).

A more practical consolidated formula:
For each runner:
- if T >= 60: contributes ceil(60 / p)
- else: contributes floor(T / p)

Input format assumed:
n T
t1 t2 ... tn

If no runners, answer is 0.
*/

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    long long n, T;
    if (!(cin >> n >> T)) return 0;

    long long ans = 0;
    for (long long i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        long long p;
        cin >> p;
        if (p <= 0) continue;

        if (T >= 60) {
            ans += (60 + p - 1) / p; // ceil(60 / p)
        } else {
            ans += T / p; // floor(T / p)
        }
    }

    cout << ans << '\n';
    return 0;
}

